THE 7-WALL CRUMB LAW (exact, fee-scale windows):
  300 windows at L = 3/(2V): dead (fully covered) = 1, live = 299  (100% live)
  rescuing-pair gcd distribution: {3: 17, 1: 174, 2: 43, '5+': 57, 4: 7}
  rescuing-pair adjacency: {'non-adj': 188, 'adjacent': 110}
  rescuing-pair ratio: median 1.56, p90 3.44

  scan: dead-window FRACTION per block (how much of the circle is dead
  at fee scale -- the window-choice freedom upstream must beat this):
  25 blocks x 140 positions: dead fraction median 0.01, max 0.07, min 0.00

  -> if max dead-fraction < 1: window choice ALWAYS exists at fee
     scale; the 7-wall crumb is a POSITIONAL pigeonhole, not a
     per-window analytic bound.
